As we can see, the PexObserve.ValueForViewing is a way to pass certain values to the Pex Visualization when asserting method contracts. Pex will be able to visualize these values in its UI, which helps debugging. I'm not sure what the purpose of the AssumePrecondition.IsTrue() is doing exactly, but the general idea seems to be making sure that conditions are always true for the tests.

What are these assume preconditions for?

In the code example given, we can see that the assume preconditions are often asserting conditions for tests in an if (PexAssumeUnderTest.IsTrue(...)) block, but it's not clear what they serve as a purpose exactly.

A: The assume preconditions in Pex tests are in place for controlling the search space during symbolic execution. They help in limiting the search space significantly to only those states where the condition is true. If the assume precondition is removed, Pex will need to explore all the states which could leave it in an unsafe state that would never be executed in a real-world program but could be part of the test.

This way Pex takes the guesswork out of testing where the developer would have to manually filter out all the likely false states while testing.

In the context of assume preconditions like PexAssumeUnderTest.IsTrue(true), the purpose is to give Pex a way to assert that some condition is always satisfied during the execution of tests. If Pex ever needs to explore a path where that condition is not satisfied, Pex is not going to explore that path. This is mainly to make Pex's symbolic execution more efficient.

But often in real-world applications there might be some conditions that are always true and don't cause any issues or failures, but they are not always true for all inputs. In these cases, the assume precondition helps to limit the symbolic execution even more if a certain condition could not be always true.

So in this case assume preconditions are helping to limit the space even further where the condition is checked in every function call. 

But what I understand is that you meant for PexAssumeUnderTest.IsTrue(true). But this is not the case, you used PexAssumeUnderTest.IsTrue(true), also PexAssumeUnderTest.IsTrue(false) might be used depending on the state of the execution.
Normally assume precondition would look something--------------------            Debug.Assert(!(New_Ret < 0) || Old_arrListCount >= 0);
	}
